1pa3|v|To lift up; to raise again, as one who has fallen; to cause to rise. ; To cause to seem to rise; to put in relief; to give prominence or conspicuousness to; to set off by contrast. ; To raise up something in; to introduce a contrast or variety into; to remove the monotony or sameness of. ; To raise or remove, as anything which depresses, weighs down, or crushes; to render less burdensome or afflicting; to alleviate; to abate; to mitigate; to lessen; as, to relieve pain; to relieve the wants of the poor. ; To free, wholly or partly, from any burden, trial, evil, distress, or the like; to give ease, comfort, or consolation to; to give aid, help, or succor to; to support, strengthen, or deliver; as, to relieve a besieged town. ; To release from a post, station, or duty; to put another in place of, or to take the place of, in the bearing of any burden, or discharge of any duty. ; To ease of any imposition, burden, wrong, or oppression, by judicial or legislative interposition, as by the removal of a grievance, by indemnification for losses, or the like; to right.	o:13hc,106,1;et2,104,4;1fse,76,7;1y71,70,2;1yba,65,4;1le1,58,17~1le1,58,17;1fse,76,7;1yba,65,4;et2,104,4;1y71,70,2;13hc,106,1	s:1yba,65,4~1yba,65,4	d:2cj,6,6~2cj,6,6
